# Living Survey: Quantum_Computing                                                                                   
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
## Introduction                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                     
Recent research continues to explore the fundamental boundaries of quantum mechanics and computational paradigms. A  
notable proposal introduces atom computing, suggesting that controlled flows of single electrons or photons could    
replace traditional transistors and qubits, offering a deterministic switching mechanism that bypasses current       
scaling constraints [^10.34257_gjsfra257362]. Broader surveys continue to map the expansive realms of quantum        
computing applications, emphasizing its counterintuitive yet powerful reliance on superposition and entanglement to  
disrupt classical digital logic [^10.5120_ijca2021921834]. Furthermore, comparative analyses of future computing     
paradigms highlight quantum systems alongside digital and biological architectures, stressing the need for           
standardized engineering descriptions to bridge theoretical potential with practical implementation                  
[^10.1109_mc.2021.3083430].                                                                                          
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
## Quantum Algorithms and Simulation                                                                                 
                                                                                                                     
The simulation of complex physical and chemical systems remains a primary driver for quantum algorithm development.  
Practical guides have emerged for applying quantum annealing to materials science, demonstrating how defective       
graphene structures can be modeled on quantum annealers to calculate relative energies intractable for classical     
computers [^10.1063_5.0151346]. In the realm of fluid dynamics, quantum computing is being adapted for Computational 
Fluid Dynamics (CFD) simulations, with approaches ranging from solving Navier-Stokes equations to implementing       
Lattice Boltzmann methods for 2D flow modeling around industrial components                                          
[^10.11159_icffts23.133][^10.21741_9781644902813-38]. Additionally, specialized software like PULSEE has been        
developed to simulate magnetic resonance observables, leveraging quantum information science protocols to investigate
emergent quantum order in strongly correlated materials [^10.1016_j.cpc.2022.108598]. Optimization problems also     
benefit from quantum approaches, where hybrid granular computing schemes enhance quantum genetic algorithms to       
navigate continuous search spaces more effectively on current noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) devices        
[^10.1007_s41066-022-00348-9].                                                                                       
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
## Quantum Communication and Cryptography                                                                            
                                                                                                                     
Secure communication networks are undergoing a paradigm shift with the advent of quantum internet technologies.      
Primer studies outline the theoretical infrastructure required for a global quantum internet, promising ultra-secure 
connectivity and transformative capabilities for industries reliant on data transmission [^10.31695_ijerat.2025.9.1].
At the protocol level, academic works provide detailed implementations and security analyses of quantum key          
distribution (QKD) schemes, including BB84, B92, and B91, while evaluating vulnerabilities like intercept-resend and 
faked-state attacks [^10.6084_m9.figshare.32051724][https:__openalex.org_W7112574406]. The cryptographic implications
of quantum computing are equally critical, with advanced hybrid encryption systems integrating post-quantum          
algorithms to counter threats from Grover’s and Shor’s algorithms [^10.56975_tijer.v13i3.161368]. Concurrently,      
cryptanalysis reports rigorously evaluate the security of post-quantum key exchange schemes like Kyber and Dilithium,
demonstrating how improved dual lattice attacks reduce their effective security margins [^10.5281_zenodo.6493704].   
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                     
## Quantum Ecosystem and Toolchains                                                                                  
                                                                                                                     
As quantum technologies transition from theoretical research to industrial adoption, understanding the developer     
ecosystem becomes paramount. Empirical investigations mining developer discussions reveal that hybrid                
quantum-classical computing and circuit implementation dominate practitioner conversations, with frameworks like     
Qiskit and Q# leading the landscape [^https:__openalex.org_W7155247427]. Tracking emerging trends through open-source
repositories and financial news further illustrates the growing momentum of quantum technologies in both academic and
commercial sectors [^10.1007_978-3-031-90727-2_20].                                                                  
                                                                                                                     

